# "A Five-Minute Introduction to StoryCAD" (#1421 Milestone 5), from the brief Terry approved
# 2026-10-14 (StoryCADTests/AutomationScripts/Intro-Video-brief.md). One step per brief row.
# Test profile: a regression check of the steps the video shows.
# Video: StoryCADAutomation voice Intro-Video.scs --out <dir>, then
#        StoryCADAutomation video Intro-Video.scs --out <dir> --assets <dir>
# The assets folder holds "The Old Man and the Sea.stbx", Terry's filled-in copy of the sample
# (the shipped sample has empty tabs); video copies it to {scratch}/outlines (#1626, 2026-10-20).
# Test run: StoryCADAutomation run Intro-Video.scs --assets <dir>, with the same assets folder.
script "Intro Video"
title "A Five-Minute Introduction to StoryCAD"
launch                                    # the recording starts here; the cards cover the app
expect FileOpenNav exists

step "An outline"
narrate "StoryCAD is a free and open source computer-aided design tool for fiction writers. It helps you build an outline: a working plan of your story that you develop before and during the draft, where an idea costs nothing to try or to discard."
show text "An outline is a plan of your story."

step "Story elements"
narrate "An outline in StoryCAD is made of story elements. The story overview describes the work as a whole. Problems are the conflicts that drive the plot. Characters, settings and scenes are what you would expect, and a scene ties together a setting, a cast and an event."
show text "STORY ELEMENTS\n\nStory Overview\nProblem\nCharacter\nSetting\nScene"   # \n: a new line; \n\n sets the heading apart

step "The outline file"
narrate "Each outline is a single file with the extension dot S-T-B-X, which you can copy, move or back up like any other document. StoryCAD ships with sample outlines; this one is The Old Man and the Sea."
click OpenFromFileNavItem                 # the Open dialog is up from launch
open-file-dialog "{scratch}/outlines/The Old Man and the Sea.stbx"
expect tree contains "The Old Man and the Sea"

step "The navigation pane" hold 1
narrate "The navigation pane on the left shows the outline as a tree. Each node is a story element, with the story overview at the top and everything else beneath it."

step "The content pane"
narrate "Selecting a node opens that element in the content pane on the right. Santiago is a character, so his page is a character form."
open-node tree "The Old Man and the Sea/Characters/Santiago"

step "Tabs"
narrate "Larger forms are divided into tabs, each covering one aspect of the element: for a character, role, appearance, backstory and more. Every major form also has a Notes tab."
pause 2
tab AppearanceTab
pause 2
tab BackstoryTab

step "The menu bar"
narrate "The menu bar across the top opens and saves outlines, adds and removes elements, runs the tools and prints reports."
pause 3
click PreferencesButton

step "Preferences"
narrate "Preferences hold your name, your default outline folder and your backup and autosave settings. Your name becomes the author of each new outline."
pause 2
dialog "Preferences" click name:"Cancel"

step "A new outline"
narrate "New outlines start from a template. This one uses the blank template, and the project name, Fish Story, becomes the file name."
menu OpenCreateFileMenuItem
click CreateNewOutlineNavItem
set FileOpenProjectNameTextBox "Fish Story"
press "Tab"                               # the name binding updates only when focus leaves the box
click PrimaryButton                       # "Create outline"
expect tree contains "Fish Story"

step "A blank outline" hold 1
narrate "A blank outline has two nodes: the story overview, named for the story, and the trash can, which holds deleted elements until you restore or empty them."

step "The story idea"
narrate "Work usually begins on the overview. The Story Idea tab records the idea that started it all."
tab StoryIdeaTab
focus StoryIdeaRichEdit                  # RichEdit boxes have no Value pattern, so focus and type
type "Codify a local urban legend as a morality tale."

step "The concept"
narrate "The Concept tab turns that idea into a question with conflict built in. “What if” is a good way in."
tab ConceptTab
focus ConceptRichEdit
type "What if a morally upstanding man failed utterly and repeatedly?"

step "Adding story elements"
narrate "You add story elements from the menu bar or by right-clicking a node in the tree. Here, a problem, the struggle that drives the story, and two characters, Santiago and Manolin."
context-menu tree "Fish Story" "Add Elements/Add Problem"
open-node tree "Fish Story/New Problem"
set ProblemNameTextBox "An old man hasn't caught a fish in 84 days"
expect tree contains "Fish Story/An old man hasn't caught a fish in 84 days"
click tree "Fish Story"                   # the root row has no Invoke; click falls back to a real pointer
menu AddCharacterMenuItem
open-node tree "Fish Story/New Character"
set CharacterNameTextBox "Santiago"
click tree "Fish Story"
menu AddCharacterMenuItem
open-node tree "Fish Story/New Character"
set CharacterNameTextBox "Manolin"
expect tree contains "Fish Story/Santiago"
expect tree contains "Fish Story/Manolin"

step "Any order" hold 1
narrate "There is no required order. Add elements as they occur to you, or work from beginning to end. Each one waits in the tree until you return to it."

step "Master Plots"
narrate "StoryCAD's plotting aids are on the Tools menu. Master Plots offers patterns that stories have used for centuries, with notes on each. Copying Ascent/Descent adds its structure and suggested scenes to the outline."
click tree "Fish Story"
menu MasterPlotsMenuItem
select MasterPlotsCombo "Ascent/Descent"
pause 2
click PrimaryButton                       # "Copy"
expect StatusMessageText text "MasterPlot Ascent/Descent inserted"
expect tree contains "Fish Story/Rise and Fall"   # one of the three scenes it inserts

step "Reports"
narrate "When you are ready to draft, print reports on any part of the outline."
menu PrintReportsMenuItem
toggle PrintReportsOverviewCheck on
tab PrintReportsCharactersTab
toggle PrintReportsCharacterListCheck on
pause 1
dialog "Generate Reports" click name:"Cancel"

step "Scrivener"
narrate "If you draft in Scrivener, StoryCAD can export the outline into a Scrivener project, one document per story element."
click ReportsMenuButton
expect ScrivenerReportsMenuItem exists
pause 3
press "Escape"

step "Where to get StoryCAD"
narrate "StoryCAD is free from the Microsoft Store and the Mac App Store. Thanks for watching."
pause 6                                   # the app stays on screen while the last line plays
menu ExitMenuItem
dialog "Save changes?" click name:"No"
expect-exit

closing "Like and subscribe"
